# Carb entries

A carb entry is the simplest record in the food system: an amount of
carbohydrate, in grams, at a moment in time. Everything else in this section
hangs off it.

## What one holds

| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| **Carbs** | Grams of carbohydrate. The only required value. |
| **Time** | When the carbs were eaten, stored to the millisecond. |
| **Absorption time** | Minutes. How long this food takes to absorb, when the source knows. |
| **Carb time** | Minutes of offset, used by some uploaders to say "these carbs are for later". |
| **Fat** and **protein** | Grams, when the source reports them. |
| **Source and device** | Which app or pump the entry came from. |

<Screenshot id="carb-entry-edit" />

Fat and protein are real fields, not carbs in disguise. Some older uploaders
convert fat and protein into a series of invented carb entries spread over the
following hours — the fat-protein-unit trick. Where a source reports the macros
properly, Nocturne stores them as macros, so your carb history stays a record of
carbs.

### Absorption time

Loop, AAPS and similar systems attach an absorption time to a carb entry when
you tell them a meal is slow or fast. When it is present it overrides the
default from your profile for carbs-on-board purposes. When it is absent — which
is most of the time, and always for carbs typed in by hand — the profile default
applies.

## Where carb entries come from

Four routes, all producing the same kind of record:

- **You log them in Nocturne.** Typed in against the graph or as part of a meal.
- **Your loop or pump uploads them.** Loop, AAPS, Trio and the rest send carbs
  with the bolus you gave for them.
- **A connector imports them.** Glooko, Tidepool, MyLife, Twiist, a Nightscout
  instance you are migrating from, and others.
- **An app posts a Nightscout-style treatment.** Anything written against the
  legacy `/api/v1/treatments` or `/api/v3/treatments` surface still works.

That last one is worth expanding. A legacy Nightscout treatment is one document
that may carry carbs, insulin, a blood glucose check and a note all at once.
Nocturne takes it apart into its constituent records, so a "Meal Bolus" with
`carbs: 45, insulin: 4.5` becomes a carb entry and a bolus, correlated with each
other. Read it back through the legacy API and you get your treatment document
again, reassembled.

<Callout type="info" title="Sync identifiers">
  Loop and AAPS give every record they upload a stable sync identifier. Nocturne
  keys on it, so if the same app re-uploads the same carb entry — after a phone
  restore, a backfill, or a flaky connection — the existing record is updated
  rather than a second one created. This is exact-key matching between one app
  and itself; the fuzzier problem of <em>two different</em> apps reporting the
  same meal is covered in
  <a href="/docs/food/deduplication">When the same carbs arrive twice</a>.
</Callout>

## Editing and deleting

Carb entries are editable after the fact, from the graph or the **Meals** page.
Correcting the amount is the usual reason: you guessed 40 g at the table and
worked out later it was closer to 55 g.

Deleting one is a real delete from your point of view, but Nocturne keeps a
tombstone behind the scenes. That tombstone is what stops a connector's next
sync from cheerfully re-importing the entry you just deleted. A record that
Nocturne itself swept away — as opposed to one you deleted — carries no such
block, so ordinary resyncs still work.

## Through the API

Carb entries live under `/api/v4/nutrition/carbs`, with the usual list, fetch,
create, update and delete, plus a bulk create for importers and a delete keyed
on a source's own sync identifier for apps that never learned Nocturne's IDs.

They are gated by the `treatments.read` and `treatments.readwrite` scopes, the
same as boluses — a share link or connected app that can see treatments can see
carbs. The food catalog is a separate category with its own scopes; see
[the food catalog](/docs/food/catalog).
